  "why": "One shape per rule of the CommonJS export tiers (js-review-followup D5: every rule moves a gated corpus metric, not only a unit test). A `Reflect.defineProperty` write and a write inside a declaration (`var all = exports.list = list`) are exports; top-level `this.find =` writes `exports`; `__proto__` in the exported literal and `Object.create` of a local prototype make members callable that the file never writes, so those modules are resolved by name; a getter is an export whose value this pass cannot name, resolved by name; `exports.stale` after `module.exports = make()` is written onto an object nobody exports. `util.util()` through `const util = require('./util')` is the member `util`, not the whole module. And a `var` in a class expression's `static {}` block is the block's own. Each `secret` is declared and never exported.",
